course: dictionary
lesson: 14251
title: Animal sounds: neigh
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a sound. Some animals have their own special sound. A cow says moo, a sheep says baa, and a horse says neigh. When you read a story, the sound tells you what the animal is doing and how it feels.
explain: «The horse gave a loud neigh when it saw the apples.» Here neigh is a noun, a thing you can hear. The horse made a loud, high call, so it must have been excited about the apples.
explain: «The horse began to neigh in the field.» Here neigh is a verb, an action word. Ask yourself: what is the horse doing? It is making its long, high call. Try the sound in the sentence to check: the horse began to moo does not fit a horse, so neigh is the right word.
fact: Neigh can be a verb or a noun. As a verb: «The horse neighs.» As a noun: «The horse gave a neigh.»
fact: A neigh is the long, high, loud call a horse makes.
fact: «Neigh loudly» and «a loud neigh» are phrases you will often see in stories.
fact: In a story, a neigh can show that a horse is excited, frightened or calling to other horses.
fact: Cows moo, sheep baa, horses neigh: each animal has its own sound word.
exercise: «The horse gave a loud neigh.» What is a neigh? | *The sound a horse makes | A kind of hay | A big jump over a fence | A cow's soft moo
exercise: «The horse neighed when it saw its friend coming.» What does neighed mean? | The horse ate its dinner | The horse ran away very fast | *The horse made its long, high call | The horse lay down to sleep
exercise: Copy the word from this sentence that means a horse's call: «The pony gave a happy neigh and trotted to the gate.» | pony | trotted | gate | *neigh
test: Which word completes the sentence? Cows moo, sheep baa, and horses ... | *neigh | hop | quack | oink
test: Which animal neighs? | Sheep | Cow | Duck | *Horse
exam: «In the story, the horse neighed loudly, so the farmer knew it was frightened.» What does the neigh tell the reader? | *The horse is frightened or calling to other horses | The farmer is singing | The horse is sleeping | The horse has eaten too much hay
